-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- Candidate 1 — CRITICAL, row_type incident.
-- THE EYE's three dense-motion gates ALL pass, by construction, on a scene that
-- never moved at all. Measured: Desk B ran two concepts to 35/35 PASS whose
-- frames are MD5-identical across t=0 / t=5000 / t=10000 / frozen.
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
INSERT INTO engine_bug_queue (
    bug_class, title, severity, owner_cluster, root_cause, prevention_rule,
    probe_type, probe_logic, status, concepts_affected, fixed_in_files,
    discovered_in_session, row_type
) VALUES (
    'eye_dense_motion_gates_all_pass_by_construction_on_a_totally_static_scene',
    'A field_3d concept that never moves a single pixel passes every one of THE EYE''s dense-motion gates and reports all-green — the three gates are individually reasonable and jointly cannot detect total failure',
    'CRITICAL',
    'peter_parker:visual_validator',
    'THE EYE has exactly three dense-motion gates and a fully static scene defeats all three at once, each for a different and individually defensible reason. (1) [D5] "Dense motion present" is the only gate that asserts pixels moved, and it runs ONLY when deriveMotionExpectations returned true for the state; otherwise it takes the else branch at pixelGate.ts:302-305 and reports "Skipped — motion expectation unknown", counted as a PASS. deriveMotionExpectations declares field_3d motion per scenario_type, and has NO newtons_laws_body branch — deliberately, and documented as such at deriveStateMeta.ts:2880 ("nothing is asserted about a repeating state''s pixels ... Nothing to fix in either derivation"). So for every nlb concept, the one gate that could catch a dead scene abstains. (2) [D7] "No stuck tail" computes stuck = tailFrozen && earlierMoved (pixelGate.ts:320-322). earlierMoved is diffs.slice(0,-tailPairs).some(d => d >= 0.001). In a totally static scene NOTHING moved, so earlierMoved is FALSE, so stuck is FALSE, and the gate reports the actively misleading "OK — no frozen tail". D7 is built to catch an animation that DIES mid-state; it requires the scene to have lived first, so the worse defect — never having started — is invisible to it. (3) [D6] "No mid-state pixel teleport" gates its whole search on med > DENSE_TELEPORT_MIN_MEDIAN (0.001) at pixelGate.ts:309; a static series has median 0, so spikeIdx is -1 and it passes. Net: zero motion produces three green D-rows plus a green headline. Measured by Desk B on two rolling concepts: 35 of 35 checks PASS while MD5 over the dense captures returns ONE hash across t=0, t=5000, t=10000 and the frozen pin. The composition is the defect — no single gate is wrong on its own terms, which is why this survived review. This is the same silent-abstention family the chapter has now hit four times: the unknown-readouts-token skip (field_3d_renderer.ts:50163), the NaN guard in the rbr restart anchor (:49918), the starved motion map (findings_c PASS 14), and this.',
    'A gate that DECLINES to run must never be counted as a gate that PASSED. Three changes, and the first is the load-bearing one. (a) Add an absolute-floor stillness assertion that does not depend on expectsMotion: if EVERY adjacent pair in the dense series is below DENSE_MOTION_EPSILON for the WHOLE state — not merely the tail — that is a dead scene, and it must FAIL (or at the very least emit a loud non-pass status) regardless of whether motion was declared. A concept whose every frame is byte-identical is never a correct result. (b) THE EYE must not print an all-green summary while gates are abstaining: report "N passed, M skipped" and refuse the clean-bill headline when the motion map is entirely "?" — a skip count next to the pass count would have exposed this immediately, and the same fix answers findings_c PASS 14''s open office question. (c) The general rule, which is the same lesson F-C8 taught on the L-arrow probe: PAIR EVERY DELTA ASSERTION WITH AN ABSOLUTE FLOOR. D5, D6 and D7 are all delta assertions; not one of them can distinguish "it changed acceptably" from "there is nothing there to change". Note this row does NOT ask for a newtons_laws_body branch in deriveMotionExpectations — that omission is deliberate and correct, and adding one to paper over this would over-declare motion on legitimately repeating states.',
    'js_eval',
    'Construct a dense frame series of N >= 8 byte-identical PNGs (any real capture duplicated is sufficient) and run the pixelGate dense block over it with expectsMotion undefined and holdExpectation undefined — i.e. exactly the nlb path. Assert that the returned results do NOT contain a passing [D5], [D6] and [D7] triple. Today all three pass: [D5] "Skipped — motion expectation unknown", [D6] "OK — no adjacent pair exceeds max(20%, 8x median=0.00%)", [D7] "OK — no frozen tail". Second leg, end to end: seed any newtons_laws_body concept whose scene is static, run npm run visual:eyes, and assert the headline is not all-pass. Cross-check with md5sum over the STATE_n__dense_t*.png set — a single hash across the series is proof of a dead scene, and is the check that caught this.',
    'OPEN',
    ARRAY['pure_rolling', 'rolling_on_incline']::text[],
    ARRAY['src/lib/validators/visual/pixelGate.ts', 'src/scripts/visual_eyes.ts']::text[],
    'rotmech Phase 0c-3 — Desk E frozen-scope build 2026-08-05',
    'incident'
);


-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- Candidate 2 — CRITICAL, row_type incident. Dispatch E2.
-- The newtons_laws_body rolling branch (SEAM R) had a DYNAMIC availability gate
-- and no KINEMATIC one, so on flat ground it adopted rolling on frame 1 for a
-- body whose contact was demonstrably sliding. FIXED in this dispatch.
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
INSERT INTO engine_bug_queue (
    bug_class, title, severity, owner_cluster, root_cause, prevention_rule,
    probe_type, probe_logic, status, concepts_affected, fixed_in_files,
    discovered_in_session, row_type
) VALUES (
    'nlb_rolling_branch_has_no_kinematic_gate',
    'A rolling body on FLAT ground adopts the rolling branch on frame 1 no matter what its spin is — the gate tests only whether the friction rolling demands is available, which on flat ground is the vacuous 0 <= mu_s*N',
    'CRITICAL',
    'peter_parker:field3d_surgeon',
    'SEAM R gated the rolling branch on ONE test — canRoll = |f_roll| <= mu_s*N — which is a DYNAMIC availability question ("is the friction rolling demands available?"). On an incline that is the complete and correct gate: drive = m*g*sin(theta) is nonzero, so it reduces exactly to mu_s >= (k/(1+k))*|tan theta|, which is what the seam header derives. On FLAT ground drive is identically 0, so a_roll = drive/(m*(1+k)) = 0 and f_roll = -k*m*a_roll = 0 on EVERY frame REGARDLESS of the body''s actual (v, omega), and the test degenerates to 0 <= mu_s*N — true for any mu_s >= 0. Nothing anywhere in the branch tested the KINEMATIC rolling condition v = omega*R. A wheel launched sliding (v0 = 2.0 m/s, omega0 = 0) therefore took the rolling branch on frame 1, with four traced consequences: (1) a = a_roll = 0 and f = f_roll = 0, so v never decelerated and the friction readout showed a dishonest 0.00 N beside a contact speed of 2.00 m/s; (2) the omega = v/R line is guarded by !_spinIndep, and authoring omega0_rad_s sets _spinIndep, so omega fell to nlbRollSpin''s lazily-created default segment whose alpha is 0 and stayed FROZEN at its seed forever; (3) _slipping is only ever set inside the kinetic branch, which rollHeld pre-empted, so the capture re-anchor could never fire; (4) the alternative authoring (omega0_rad_s with no rolling flag) was an equally dead end, because the kinetic branch''s two halves disagree about what a slipping body is — vRef is contact-relative under (rolling || _spinIndep) but the angular sub-block that integrates omega was gated on rolling ALONE. Net: a wheel authored per the approved design glided at constant v with its spin frozen, no deceleration, no spin-up, no capture. Measured (standalone port of the branch, fixed 1/60 s steps): v held at 2.0000 m/s and R*omega at 0.0000 for the entire run. A SECOND defect surfaced the moment the gate was added: a plain tolerance band |v - omega*R| < NLB_STOP_EPS_V (0.01 m/s) is NARROWER THAN ONE STEP of contact closure — 0.0245 m/s at a single 1/60 s step for pure_rolling STATE_7, 0.0706 m/s at the 3-step dtStep the master clock hands over on a slow frame — so the branch steps straight over the crossing and the contact chatters about zero forever, never rolling. Measured on the STATE_8 slider grid: 76 of 126 (omega0, mu_k, step-size) combinations NEVER captured with a band test alone. THE EYE cannot see any of this: deriveMotionExpectations has no newtons_laws_body branch, so [D5] abstains, and a scene that never moved defeats [D6] and [D7] by construction (see Candidate 1 in this file).',
    'A CONSTRAINT BRANCH NEEDS BOTH ITS GATES — the dynamic one (are the constraint forces available?) AND the kinematic one (is the constraint currently SATISFIED?). Checking only availability is safe wherever the drive term is nonzero and silently vacuous wherever it is zero, which is exactly the flat-ground case the concept was built to teach; a gate that reduces to 0 <= x is not a gate. Corollaries, each of which cost a separate diagnosis here: (a) a tolerance band on a quantity that is being driven to zero must be compared against |rate|*dt, not chosen for readability — where the per-step change can exceed the band, pair the band with a CROSSING test (does this step carry the quantity through zero?), the same idiom the translational rest test in this integrator already uses; (b) when two halves of one physical transition are gated separately, the gates must be spelled IDENTICALLY — vRef used (rolling || _spinIndep), the angular integrator used rolling, and the disagreement made one authoring path silently half-work; (c) a capture must SNAP the constrained quantity, not merely re-anchor at the pre-capture value: with alpha = a/R and dv/dt = a the two stay exactly parallel, so any gap present at the anchor never closes (measured: contact pinned at 0.0087 m/s forever, i.e. a HUD reading 0.01 under a caption claiming the contact is at rest); (d) a gate that reads a live quantity must read a SEED-SAFE one — b.omega is 0 at apply while b.omega0 carries the authored seed, and a gate reading b.omega alone mis-branches on frame 1 and loses the authored spin permanently.',
    'js_eval',
    'Port the Branch A inner loop (the rolling gate, the capture block, the kinetic branch and nlbRollSpin) into a standalone driver and run the flat launch: theta 0, m 1, R 0.25, k 0.5 (wheel), v0 2.0, omega0 0, mu_s = mu_k = 0.05. ASSERT (1) v strictly DECREASES and R*omega strictly INCREASES on every pre-capture frame; (2) they converge at the analytic t_c = v0*k/(mu_k*g*(1+k)) = 1360.5 ms — capture must land within one step of it; (3) after capture max|contact| == 0 and max|f| == 0 for the rest of the run; (4) the capture survives every step size the master clock produces — repeat with h in {1/60, [0.016,0.032], 0.048, [0.016,0.048,0.032]} and assert capture never fails; (5) sweep the STATE_8 sliders (omega0 0..7.2 x mu_k 0.02..0.4 x both clocks) and assert capture occurs and does not flip back in every cell. BACK-COMPAT leg, which is the one that must be run on every future edit to this branch: replay EVERY body of EVERY state of rolling_on_incline and pure_rolling through both the pre-change and post-change functions for 300 frames and diff t/rollHeld/stuck/a/f/v/s/omega/theta bitwise — the ONLY body-state permitted to differ is the one being fixed. Measured here: 25 of 26 body-states bit-identical under both the fixed 1/60 clock and the variable 0.016/0.032/0.048 clock, the sole DIFF being pure_rolling/STATE_7/wheel; rolling_on_incline was 17 of 17 identical INCLUDING its STATE_7 mu_s param_ramp slipping state. DETERMINISM leg: re-running from t=0 to frame N must reproduce frame N bit-for-bit, and five consecutive dt=0 pin frames (mid-slip AND post-capture) must leave every quantity bit-identical — no latch.',
    'FIXED',
    ARRAY['pure_rolling', 'rolling_on_incline']::text[],
    ARRAY['src/lib/renderers/field_3d_renderer.ts']::text[],
    'rotmech Phase 0c-3 — Desk E dispatch E2 2026-08-05',
    'incident'
);


-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- Candidate 3 — CRITICAL, row_type incident. Dispatch E3.
-- SEAM R widened the controls_visible token enum by three dials and the WIRING
-- never landed; a token with no NLB_SLIDER_SPEC row is dropped in total silence.
-- FIXED in this dispatch.
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
INSERT INTO engine_bug_queue (
    bug_class, title, severity, owner_cluster, root_cause, prevention_rule,
    probe_type, probe_logic, status, concepts_affected, fixed_in_files,
    discovered_in_session, row_type
) VALUES (
    'nlb_seam_r_slider_tokens_declared_but_unwired',
    'Three controls_visible tokens were added to the TypeScript enum and to nothing else — the states that author them ship with no live control at all, and the engine drops the token without a word',
    'CRITICAL',
    'peter_parker:field3d_surgeon',
    'SEAM R (rotmech 0c-2) widened the newtons_laws_body controls_visible union by ''R'', ''R2'' and ''omega0'' and documented them in the interface comment, but NLB_SLIDER_TOKENS and NLB_SLIDER_SPEC were left at their eight pre-SEAM-R entries and nlbApplyParam / nlbSliderValueFromEngine carried no branch for any of the three. The token enum is a TypeScript type: it is erased at build and constrains nothing at runtime, so the only runtime reader is nlbSliderTokensUsed''s filter `if (NLB_SLIDER_SPEC[cv[c]]) want[cv[c]] = true;` — which DROPS an unrecognised token with no row, no disabled row, no console message and no gate failure. A state whose ONLY authored control was such a token therefore rendered a panel with display:none and read exactly like a state the author had deliberately given no controls. Measured blast radius: pure_rolling STATE_1 (controls_visible ["R"], its only control) shipped with zero live controls and the authored "R drag re-lifts, re-scales and respaces the marks + bracket" mechanism was unreachable; STATE_8''s ["v0","R","omega0","mu_k"] silently reduced to v0 + mu_k; rolling_on_incline S4''s physics-block-mandated live R2 and S8''s radius dials were inert. A SECOND defect sat underneath the first and would have made the wiring alone a dial that moves nothing: the drawn radius is BUILD-TIME geometry. SEAM G builds a wheel''s tyre/hub/spokes at the constant NLB_WHEEL_R while the body''s LIFT (mesh.userData._liftY) and its spin divisor (_spinR) were already the body''s own radius_m, so a wheel authoring radius_m 0.25 was drawn at 0.55 m and stood on a 0.25 m axle height — it sank through the track before any slider existed, and the first touch of a live R dial would have SNAPPED it from its build size to its physical size. The revolution marks, the circumference bracket, the centre markers and the contact layer all re-read nlbRadiusM/nlbRadiusW off the live record every frame and needed nothing. THE EYE cannot see any of this: it cannot fire a trusted drag at all, so a slider is structurally outside its reach, and for every newtons_laws_body concept deriveMotionExpectations abstains ([D5] skips — see Candidate 1 in this file).',
    'A TOKEN ENUM WIDENED IN THE TYPE SYSTEM IS NOT A FEATURE — the same commit must land the runtime spec row, the write branch and the read-back branch, or the type is documentation of something that does not exist. Corollaries, each of which cost a separate diagnosis here: (a) a lookup that FILTERS unknown keys must say so — this defect was invisible for exactly as long as it was silent, and a one-line console.warn in the union pass would have exposed it the first time the concept loaded (the same silent-abstention family as Candidate 1, the unknown-readouts-token skip and the starved motion map); (b) a control whose value is consumed by BUILD-TIME geometry needs that geometry re-resolved on the write AND at state entry AND at build from the same one function — re-scaling only on the write leaves the build pose and the first drag pose disagreeing, and re-scaling without re-lifting leaves the wheel sunk into the track, which is the same defect one layer in; (c) a live control that is also an INITIAL CONDITION writes the live quantity AND the replay seed (the v0 precedent: RESET_TRAJECTORY re-seeds from the seed field, so a one-sided write is stomped by the next replay); (d) a control that feeds a GATE must write every field that gate can read — nlbOmegaSeeded branches on _slip0 and reads b.omega down one branch and b.omega0 down the other, so a spin write that touched only one would leave the rolling gate branching on the old spin for as long as the other branch happened to be live; (e) a dial the engine REFUSES (radius or spin on a shape that draws no rim) is exactly as silent as a token that was never built, so the refusal warns too.',
    'js_eval',
    'Extract the emitted nlb slider + geometry surface verbatim out of FIELD_3D_RENDERER_CODE (brace-matched by function name) and run it against a stub DOM and stub meshes, for BOTH the pre-change and post-change files. ASSERT (1) a state authoring controls_visible ["R"] yields a visible, enabled row with ids nlb_r_row / nlb_r_slider / nlb_r_val (Rule 39f: the id must end in _row or the generic widget engine cannot discover it), and that the same state on the pre-change file yields panel display:none; (2) the entry read-back shows the state''s own radius and the SEED spin (b.omega is 0 at apply, so omega0 must read through nlbOmegaSeeded); (3) a trusted input on the R slider changes the engine record, multiplies the drawn group scale by the radius ratio, sets centre y to exactly radius_m * NLB_WORLD_PER_M (on the track, neither sunk nor floating), carries _spinR so s = R.theta stays true, leaves the along-track position untouched, and emits exactly one PARAM_UPDATE; (4) running the U11 marks pass at two radii moves mark #1 by exactly the circumference difference and relabels the bracket 2piR = 1.57 -> 3.14 m; (5) an omega0 write sets _spinIndep, writes b.omega AND b.omega0, re-anchors the angular segment, and is visible to the rolling gate down BOTH nlbOmegaSeeded branches (with and without an open _slip0); (6) RESET_TRAJECTORY re-seeds omega from the dragged omega0; (7) five repeat applies are byte-identical (a dt = 0 pin has no accumulator to walk); (8) an unknown token warns ONCE per distinct token instead of vanishing; (9) radius/spin writes on a block are refused and warn. BACK-COMPAT leg, the one that must run on every future edit here: replay EVERY state of EVERY committed newtons_laws_body concept through both files and diff the token list, the built panel HTML, every row''s visibility/disabled/readout, the per-body mesh position/lift/spin-divisor/group-scale and the result of every legacy token write at min/default/max — all of it must be byte-identical. Measured: 11 concepts, 58 states, 94 bodies, 0 differing.',
    'FIXED',
    ARRAY['pure_rolling', 'rolling_on_incline']::text[],
    ARRAY['src/lib/renderers/field_3d_renderer.ts']::text[],
    'rotmech Phase 0c-3 — Desk E dispatch E3 2026-08-05',
    'incident'
);


-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- Candidate 4 — CRITICAL, row_type incident. Dispatch E4.
-- The rbr angular-momentum integrator subtracted UNCONDITIONALLY, so alpha > 0
-- was unreachable at every authored value and a body seeded at rest was clamped
-- dead forever. FIXED in this dispatch.
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
INSERT INTO engine_bug_queue (
    bug_class, title, severity, owner_cluster, root_cause, prevention_rule,
    probe_type, probe_logic, status, concepts_affected, fixed_in_files,
    discovered_in_session, row_type
) VALUES (
    'rbr_torque_cannot_spin_a_body_up',
    'No torque source in the rigid_body_rotation engine could INCREASE |L| — the single integrator subtracted the torque unconditionally and took its magnitude at every assignment site, so alpha > 0 was unreachable at every authored value and a body authored at rest rendered a completely dead frame',
    'CRITICAL',
    'peter_parker:field3d_surgeon',
    'rbrLAt computed mag = Math.abs(L0) - tau * brakedSeconds, clamped it at 0, and re-applied sign(L0); eng.tau was Math.abs(...) at BOTH config assignment sites (the ''brake'' branch and the ''applied_torque'' branch) and in the live tau_brake write. The magnitude-and-subtract shape is exactly right for a friction brake, and the 0c-1 build was written to a brake concept, so it read as correct: the rest clamp acting on L (never on the derived omega) is what stops a platform and holds it. But the SIGN was derived from |L| rather than authored, which made "opposes the spin" a property of THE INTEGRATOR instead of a property of a brake, and that silently outlawed three things the enum already advertised. (1) A constant tau applied to a spinning body decayed it whatever the source string said, so ''applied_torque'' was a brake wearing another name. (2) A constant tau applied to a body at rest (omega0_rad_s: 0) gave L0 = 0, hence mag = 0 - tau*t, hence the clamp, hence L = 0 FOREVER; rbrThetaAt then integrated omega = 0 and theta never advanced — a completely static frame with a nonzero authored torque sitting on it. (3) A torque reversing a spin was impossible by construction. The consequence for the chapter was that alpha = tau/I, the atomic claim of concept 7 (tau_eq_i_alpha), could not be produced AT ALL in either direction, and Desk D did no wave-1 authoring because of it. The engine comment at the top of the scenario asserted the opposite — it called applied_torque_Nm "a constant tau_ext, which is #7''s alpha = tau/I with no extra code path", true only for the decelerating half and therefore false for the half concept 7 is about; a later desk reading that comment to decide whether it was blocked would have concluded it was not. Two adjacent gaps came from the same root and shipped in the same change: ''applied_torque'' was a LIVE branch missing from its own declared source union (so the one enum member the build implemented beyond ''brake'' was undeclared while two declared members were inert), and deriveMotionExpectations declared rbr motion FROM THE SEED ALONE (omega0 >= 0.05) — correct while the integrator could only decay, but silently skipping [D5] on precisely the rest-seeded, torque-driven states that signed torque exists to enable. A THIRD structural limit sat underneath: the engine held ONE scalar eng.tau and ONE engage window (eng.brakeOnMs / eng.brakeOffMs) written once by an either/or src branch, so a drive torque and a brake torque could not act at the same instant — the tau_net tug both Desk-D concepts want in their explore state was not a closed-form detail but a shape the engine could not express. And the omega0 slider floor was 0.5 in TWO places (RBR_SLIDER_SPEC.omega0.min and an if (!(value > 0)) return; guard in rbrApplyParam), so rest was unreachable from the sandbox even after the integrator was fixed; an AUTHORED omega0_rad_s of 0 was already honoured, which is what made the slider floor easy to miss.',
    'A SIGN THAT ENCODES PHYSICS MUST BE AUTHORED, NOT DERIVED FROM THE STATE IT ACTS ON. Deriving the sign of a torque from sign(L) (or a force from sign(v), or a current from sign(V)) hard-codes "always opposes" into the INTEGRATOR, where it silently outlaws every driving case — and it fails hardest at the fixed point, because at L = 0 the sign is meaningless and the clamp is indistinguishable from a dead simulation. Make the sign a property of the SOURCE KIND: a ''brake'' kind carries a magnitude and its one-way rest clamp; a ''drive'' kind carries an authored signed value and may carry the state through zero. Corollaries, each of which cost a separate diagnosis here: (a) AT THE FIXED POINT, NEVER CONSULT THE SIGN — define the corner explicitly as a static hold plus a breakaway condition (|tau_drive| > tau_brake), because -0 vs +0 would otherwise decide the physics and no test would name it; (b) a scenario that holds ONE scalar for a quantity several sources contribute to has a STRUCTURAL limit, not a formula limit — price it as a list plus per-source engaged windows before a concept design assumes the tug exists; (c) a closed-form integrator generalises to signed, windowed, multi-source torque WITHOUT a per-frame accumulator: walk a bounded sorted breakpoint list (each source''s engage/release, plus at most one zero crossing per segment) from the anchor on every call, which stays a pure function of t and keeps the rewind, the dt-fold and the time-pin contracts intact; (d) A MOTION DECLARATION DERIVED FROM THE SEED STOPS BEING TRUE THE MOMENT A DRIVE EXISTS — the visual gate motion map must read every term that can move the body, or it goes silent on exactly the state class the engine change was made for, and it must mirror the engine OWN breakaway condition or it over-declares the static-hold tug; (e) a floor enforced in a UI spec AND a write guard is two sites — moving one moves the number while nothing happens; (f) a contract comment that overstates what a branch implements is a defect with the same blast radius as the code, because a later desk reads it to decide whether it is blocked.',
    'js_eval',
    'Brace-match the rbr physics surface (rbrNum, rbrRAt, rbrIOf, rbrIAt, rbrCutTime, rbrEffTime, rbrRestartCount, rbrSignAt, rbrAnchor, rbrLAt, rbrLStep, rbrOmegaAt, rbrThetaAt, rbrThetaReset, rbrRestartNow, rbrApplyParam, applyRigidBodyRotationState) out of FIELD_3D_RENDERER_CODE for BOTH the pre-change and post-change files and drive them against a stub window/document, so the REAL config -> sources parsing is exercised and not a reimplementation. Every delta assertion is paired with an ABSOLUTE FLOOR, because a probe that only asserts "more than before" passes on a build that is still broken. ASSERT (1) BRAKE BYTE-IDENTITY: sweep the whole legacy brake authoring surface (tau x engage x release x omega0 x spin_sign x r) and diff L and theta with Object.is at ~145 instants per authoring — every sample must be bit-equal, INCLUDING the negative-zero anchor (omega0 = 0 with spin_sign -1), which the pre-change trailing sign multiply mapped to +0 and which a naive rewrite returns as -0; the no-torque coast must be bit-equal too. (2) SPIN-UP FROM REST, absolute: with I = 1.50 and tau = +0.75, omega(t) must equal 0.5*t EXACTLY at six instants, omega must REACH >= 4.0 rad/s, theta(8 s) must land in [15,17] rad against the analytic 0.5*alpha*t^2 = 16.00, and L(8 s) must equal 6.00 — and the SAME authoring on the pre-change file must reproduce the defect (L = 0, theta = 0). (3) SIGN IS AUTHORED: a negative drive runs omega and theta negative; a drive opposing an existing spin carries L exactly through 0 and out to -1.50, NOT clamped. (4) THE BRAKE KEEPS ITS ONE-WAY CLAMP: it reaches 0 and holds at 0 for the rest of the run, never reversing. (5) sources[]: tau_net sums (drive 2.0 + brake 0.6 -> L(3 s) = 4.20); a drive the brake outweighs gives an EXACT static hold (L, omega and theta all identically 0 for 20 s) that is IDENTICAL under spin_sign +1 and -1, which is the proof sign(L) is never read at L = 0; a spinning body under drive < brake decelerates to zero and then HOLDS rather than being dragged backwards; per-source engage/release windows are honoured independently. (6) CLOSED FORM: a dt = h vs dt = 2h fold of theta is BIT-equal at every shared instant; a 3 s -> 9 s -> 3 s -> 11 s -> 1 s -> 3 s rewind reproduces theta/L/omega exactly; an out-of-order evaluation matches the in-order series (no hysteretic latch); the static-hold branch rewinds exactly too. (7) BOTH omega0 floor sites: RBR_SLIDER_SPEC.omega0.min is 0 AND a live write of 0 sticks — and the pre-change file must drop that write. (8) the live tau_brake drag still creates and owns the brake source and is bit-equal pre vs post. (9) deriveMotionExpectations returns true for a rest-seeded state driven above the calibrated alpha floor (scalar AND sources[] forms), undefined for a brake-only rest seed, undefined for a sub-floor drive, undefined for the static-hold tug, true for a breakaway tug above the floor; deriveMaxRevealTimeMs pins past the LAST sources[] engage/release event and is unchanged on every legacy scalar authoring. LIMITS, stated because they matter: this probe reads the EMITTED SOURCE, so it proves arithmetic and parsing and proves NOTHING about pixels, camera framing, occlusion, the L arrow, the readout surfaces, or trusted-drag paths — it also cannot catch a stray backtick terminating the template literal (npm run check:renderer-syntax caught exactly that during this dispatch, one edit after the probe went green). It is a smoke test. The gate is a real EYE run on a concept that uses the scenario.',
    'FIXED',
    ARRAY['tau_eq_i_alpha', 'rotational_kinematics', 'conservation_of_angular_momentum']::text[],
    ARRAY['src/lib/renderers/field_3d_renderer.ts', 'src/lib/validators/visual/deriveStateMeta.ts']::text[],
    'rotmech Phase 0c-3 — Desk E dispatch E4 2026-08-05',
    'incident'
);


