-- Candidate 8 — CRITICAL, row_type incident.  Dispatch E11a.
-- The mechanism behind Desk B's B-3.  NOTE FOR THE FOUNDER: this row records
-- that E11a and E11b turned out to be ONE line, not two independent ones —
-- the runtime premise that split them (S7 "does not move") was wrong; S7 does
-- move, from t = 688 ms, at exactly the instant its mu_s ramp carries
-- mu_s.N below mg.sin(theta).
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
INSERT INTO engine_bug_queue (
    bug_class, title, severity, owner_cluster, root_cause, prevention_rule,
    probe_type, probe_logic, status, concepts_affected, fixed_in_files,
    discovered_in_session, row_type
) VALUES (
    'nlb_computed_motion_never_reaches_persistent_body_state',
    'The newtons_laws_body rest clamp asks the SLIDING static question of a ROLLING body, so a rolling body released from rest on an incline was clamped back to v = 0 on its first integrated frame and pinned at its seed forever -- correct f_s beside a written a = 0, with every readout that reads integrated state frozen and every readout computed from a formula moving',
    'CRITICAL',
    'peter_parker:field3d_surgeon',
    'updateNewtonsLawsBodyFrame''s Branch A ends its step with an anti-chatter clamp: if the velocity step reversed sign AND the drive cannot beat static friction, the body has come to rest, so v1 = 0 and a = 0. Both halves misfire together for a rolling body released from rest. (1) nlbSgn(0) = 0, so for v0 = 0 the sign test is true against any nonzero v1 -- it fires on the FIRST integrated frame of every body launched from rest, which is why the concept''s flat/incline split needed no second defect: pure_rolling launches every working state with v0 != 0 and never reaches the clamp. (2) The magnitude test |drive| <= mu_s.N is mu_s >= tan(theta) -- the right question for a BLOCK and the wrong one for a rolling body, whose static gate is the different and strictly weaker |f_roll| <= mu_s.N, i.e. mu_s >= (k/(1+k)).tan(theta), and which the rolling branch had ALREADY evaluated and passed a few lines earlier (canRoll). The clamp therefore asked the block question a second time and overwrote the rolling branch''s own answer. The physics it denies: a rolling body released from rest on any theta > 0 always starts moving -- it rolls if the contact can supply k/(1+k).mg.sin(theta) and slips if it cannot, but it never stays put -- so there is no rolling case the clamp can be right about. Measured on rolling_on_incline at theta = 25 deg (tan 25 = 0.46631): every body of STATE_1/2/3/4/5/6/8 authors mu_s = 0.50 or 0.90, both above tan(theta), and every one sat at its seed s = 2.400 with v = 0, omega = 0, a = 0.0000 for the whole run while f held the CORRECT rolling static friction (1.1833 / 1.3806 / 1.6567 / 2.0708 N for k = 0.4 / 0.5 / 0.667 / 1.0) -- which is exactly the reported split between readouts that read persistent state and readouts computed from a formula. STATE_7 was the single incline body that ever moved, and it moved for this reason and no other: its authored param_ramp drives mu_s 0.50 -> 0.05, and the ring released on the exact frame maxStat crossed below drive (frame 41, mu_s = 0.4676, maxStat = 4.1531 > drive = 4.1417, frozen; frame 42, t = 688 ms, mu_s = 0.4604, maxStat = 4.0892 < 4.1417, v = -0.0331, a = -2.0708). A release threshold sitting exactly on tan(theta) can come from no other line in the file. THE VALUE WAS NOT LOST IN THE INTEGRATION ITSELF: v1 = v0 + a.hPhys and the s1 trapezoid are both correct and were never the site, and neither of the two hypotheses that preceded this one was the mechanism (bodies were not held stuck by excess static friction -- the branch above ran and produced correct rolling numbers; and the clamp was refuted as an explanation only because S7 was believed not to move, which runtime disproves).',
    'A REST/CHATTER CLAMP BELONGS TO THE BRANCH THAT NEEDS IT, AND EVERY BRANCH THAT DECIDES A CONTACT REGIME OWNS ITS OWN REST TEST. (a) An anti-chatter clamp written for kinetic friction must be gated OFF on any frame a different contact branch won -- rolling, latched, driven -- because those branches have already answered the static question with their own, different criterion, and a second generic test can only overrule them. (b) TWO STATIC TESTS FOR ONE BODY IS THE SMELL: if a branch computes an availability gate (|f_required| <= mu_s.N) and a later line re-tests (|drive| <= mu_s.N), the two are different physical questions and the later one silently wins. Make the later one name which branches it applies to. (c) sign(0) IS A REST TEST IN DISGUISE: any predicate of the form sign(v0) != sign(v1) fires unconditionally on the first frame of a body launched from rest, so it can only be safe when paired with a magnitude test that is TRUE ONLY WHEN THE BODY GENUINELY CANNOT START. Audit every such predicate for the v0 = 0 case explicitly; a launch-from-rest state is the case it will be wrong about, and a concept whose working states all launch with v0 != 0 will hide it completely. (d) THE FLEET-SAFE SHAPE OF THE FIX IS A BRANCH TERM, NOT A CHANGE TO THE SHARED SIGN HELPER: nlbSgn''s zero handling is used by the coupled branch and every newtons_laws_body concept, and altering it is a fleet-wide semantic change; adding one already-computed branch flag to the clamp''s condition is provably inert for every body that does not take that branch.',
    'js_eval',
    'RUNTIME, on the REAL emitted renderer with the REAL concept config -- an emitted-code A/B cannot see this class (see byte_identical_ab_against_a_dead_baseline_reads_as_proof_of_correctness). Build the concept HTML with assembleField3DHtml (a pure function: no cache seed, no DB), drive it in headless chromium over a virtual requestAnimationFrame clock at a fixed 1/60 s step, and snapshot eng.bodies[id] every frame. LIVENESS ASSERTION, the primary gate: for every guided state, assert b.v takes MORE THAN ONE distinct value across the window, and separately that b.s and b.omega do -- a state whose persistent quantities have exactly one distinct value is dead regardless of what its force readouts say. NEGATIVE CONTROL in the same run: rolling_on_incline STATE_3 authors a rotation_locked block beside a rolling disc on the same track, so assert the block''s trajectory is bit-identical across the change (0 of 200 frames differing) while the disc''s distinct-s count goes 1 -> 108. INDEPENDENT PHYSICS, derived in the probe and never read from the renderer: a rolling body on an incline has a = g.sin(theta)/(1+k), so the four-shape race over the authored 4.5 m run must halt at sqrt(2.4.5/a) = 1744 / 1805 / 1903 / 2085 ms for k = 0.40 / 0.50 / 0.667 / 1.00; a 5.0 kg / 0.30 m sphere must TIE a 0.5 kg / 0.10 m sphere (mass and radius cancel); and the KE split at the foot of a 1.000 m drop must be 7.0 / 2.8 J for the sphere and 4.9 / 4.9 J for the ring, each summing to mgh = 9.8 J. RULE 37: the sandbox state must produce a distinct s on EVERY frame of the window (260/260), never a repeated value. RULE 36: the freeze pin must be reproducible PER STATE ENTRY -- enter the state, pin at 1500 ms, snapshot; visit another state and run it; re-enter and re-pin; assert byte-identical, three times -- and the pinned pose must hold byte-identical across 20 further frames. (Note the limit found here: within ONE state entry a pin walked BACKWARD does not rewind the integrator, because newtons_laws_body steps s and v rather than closing them in t; this is pre-existing and was demonstrated on the pre-change renderer, and it is not what THE EYE exercises, which is a fresh entry per pin.)',
    'FIXED',
